t because the plural verb &ldquo;have been&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;Josephine St. Pierre Ruffin.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-656-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"ba8cee5d-0fa3-4d3d-aae5-ddccae24b9fa","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959341,"updateDate":1691007959341}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959341, 1691007959341),
    ($SATQ$772835db$SATQ$, $SATQ$931dceb0-251c-469c-8475-e8b6fad3b219$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Most of the ice found on Earth is ice <span data-ssml-say-as="characters">Ih</span>, distinguished by a crystalline structure in which molecules form a hexagonal pattern. Amorphous ice, on the other hand, constitutes most of the ice in the ultrafrigid environment of outer space. Defined by a disorganized molecular structure, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1e670f04-9419-40df-b303-bf7992a786f3","content":"<p>ice <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ih</span> contains crystals, whereas amorphous ice, which lacks the thermal energy to form them, does not.</p>"},{"id":"938671f4-dbce-4418-afc4-056861d4cd76","content":"<p>amorphous ice lacks the thermal energy to form the crystals found in ice <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ih</span>.</p>"},{"id":"da64bc30-570d-4e72-8e01-29ce2874121f","content":"<p>the lack of thermal energy in amorphous ice explains its inability to form the crystals found in ice <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ih</span>.</p>"},{"id":"a31b56d7-dd3c-46ec-b141-dafe98ceb388","content":"<p>ice <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ih</span> differs from amorphous ice in that it possesses the thermal energy to form crystals.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["938671f4-dbce-4418-afc4-056861d4cd76"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;amorphous ice&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;defined&hellip;structure.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that amorphous ice&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;is being described as having a disorganized molecular structure.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun &ldquo;ice Ih&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that ice Ih has a disorganized molecular structure, whereas ice Ih is previously described as having molecules that form a hexagonal pattern. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the lack of thermal energy&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the lack of thermal energy has a disorganized molecular structure. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of &ldquo;ice Ih&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that ice Ih has a disorganized molecular structure, whereas ice Ih is previously described as having molecules that form a hexagonal pattern.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"772835db","external_id":"931dceb0-251c-469c-8475-e8b6fad3b219","vaultid":null,"uId":"7c248211-087a-4849-a658-f3a448830785","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Most of the ice found on Earth is ice <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ih</span>, distinguished by a crystalline structure in which molecules form a hexagonal pattern. Amorphous ice, on the other hand, constitutes most of the ice in the ultrafrigid environment of outer space. Defined by a disorganized molecular structure, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1e670f04-9419-40df-b303-bf7992a786f3","content":"<p>ice <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ih</span> contains crystals, whereas amorphous ice, which lacks the thermal energy to form them, does not.</p>"},{"id":"938671f4-dbce-4418-afc4-056861d4cd76","content":"<p>amorphous ice lacks the thermal energy to form the crystals found in ice <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ih</span>.</p>"},{"id":"da64bc30-570d-4e72-8e01-29ce2874121f","content":"<p>the lack of thermal energy in amorphous ice explains its inability to form the crystals found in ice <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ih</span>.</p>"},{"id":"a31b56d7-dd3c-46ec-b141-dafe98ceb388","content":"<p>ice <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">Ih</span> differs from amorphous ice in that it possesses the thermal energy to form crystals.</p>"}],"keys":["938671f4-dbce-4418-afc4-056861d4cd76"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;amorphous ice&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase &ldquo;defined&hellip;structure.&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that amorphous ice&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;is being described as having a disorganized molecular structure.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun &ldquo;ice Ih&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that ice Ih has a disorganized molecular structure, whereas ice Ih is previously described as having molecules that form a hexagonal pattern. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of the noun phrase &ldquo;the lack of thermal energy&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that the lack of thermal energy has a disorganized molecular structure. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of &ldquo;ice Ih&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that ice Ih has a disorganized molecular structure, whereas ice Ih is previously described as having molecules that form a hexagonal pattern.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$775f3eb9$SATQ$, $SATQ$025a5224-0751-4d35-861f-23e6d8499fb4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4a4791ff-cb0d-4fb4-8e3a-7e5e98738ae0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In his groundbreaking book <em>Bengali Harlem and the Lost Histories of South Asian America</em>, Vivek Bald uses newspaper articles, census records, ships&rsquo; logs, and memoirs to tell the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> who made New York City their home in the early twentieth century.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a641a9bb-20c2-452c-8d83-c62edd83426b","content":"<p>story&rsquo;s of the South Asian immigrants</p>"},{"id":"c632a474-77d5-4934-bec1-2c07d96a58d3","content":"<p>story&rsquo;s of the South Asian immigrants&rsquo;</p>"},{"id":"3b81e49c-bab2-4624-9586-ba87b31311cb","content":"<p>stories of the South Asian immigrants</p>"},{"id":"baadc800-14f2-42d9-9079-15eda63d74ed","content":"<p>stories&rsquo; of the South Asian immigrant&rsquo;s</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3b81e49c-bab2-4624-9586-ba87b31311cb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of plural and possessive nouns. The plural nouns &ldquo;stories&rdquo; and &ldquo;immigrants&rdquo; correctly indicate that the memoir tells multiple stories of multiple immigrants. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun &ldquo;stories,&rdquo; not the singular possessive noun &ldquo;story&rsquo;s.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the plural nouns &ldquo;stories&rdquo; and &ldquo;immigrants,&rdquo; not the singular possessive noun &ldquo;story&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural possessive noun &ldquo;immigrants&rsquo;.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the plural nouns &ldquo;stories&rdquo; and &ldquo;immigrants,&rdquo; not the plural possessive noun &ldquo;stories&rsquo;&rdquo; and the singular possessive noun &ldquo;immigrant&rsquo;s.&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-658-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$d459c613-6061-4b56-b8b1-c16161e79984$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"775f3eb9","external_id":"025a5224-0751-4d35-861f-23e6d8499fb4","vaultid":"4a4791ff-cb0d-4fb4-8e3a-7e5e98738ae0","uId":"7d2c2f9b-f70f-4f4f-97cf-7a320efa9f34","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In his groundbreaking book <em>Bengali Harlem and the Lost Histories of South Asian America</em>, Vivek Bald uses newspaper articles, census records, ships&rsquo; logs, and memoirs to tell the <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> who made New York City their home in the early twentieth century.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a641a9bb-20c2-452c-8d83-c62edd83426b","content":"<p>story&rsquo;s of the South Asian immigrants</p>"},{"id":"c632a474-77d5-4934-bec1-2c07d96a58d3","content":"<p>story&rsquo;s of the South Asian immigrants&rsquo;</p>"},{"id":"3b81e49c-bab2-4624-9586-ba87b31311cb","content":"<p>stories of the South Asian immigrants</p>"},{"id":"baadc800-14f2-42d9-9079-15eda63d74ed","content":"<p>stories&rsquo; of the South Asian immigrant&rsquo;s</p>"}],"keys":["3b81e49c-bab2-4624-9586-ba87b31311cb"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of plural and possessive nouns. The plural nouns &ldquo;stories&rdquo; and &ldquo;immigrants&rdquo; correctly indicate that the memoir tells multiple stories of multiple immigrants. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the context requires the plural noun &ldquo;stories,&rdquo; not the singular possessive noun &ldquo;story&rsquo;s.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the context requires the plural nouns &ldquo;stories&rdquo; and &ldquo;immigrants,&rdquo; not the singular possessive noun &ldquo;story&rsquo;s&rdquo; and the plural possessive noun &ldquo;immigrants&rsquo;.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because the context requires the plural nouns &ldquo;stories&rdquo; and &ldquo;immigrants,&rdquo; not the plural possessive noun &ldquo;stories&rsquo;&rdquo; and the singular possessive noun &ldquo;immigrant&rsquo;s.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-658-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"d459c613-6061-4b56-b8b1-c16161e79984","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959341,"updateDate":1691007959341}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959341, 1691007959341),
    ($SATQ$77bf77cd$SATQ$, $SATQ$b426ef5f-b7cb-4486-bd1e-c1576e4ef344$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a2555bb8-a0fa-458f-bfb0-b2099c413fb9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Farouk El-Baz, a geologist and space scientist, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> part of the team that selected the lunar landing sites for the Apollo program during the 1960s and 1970s.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a5a54cf8-21b4-4b93-bd9f-41a768b77772","content":"<p>are</p>"},{"id":"9d6b1c27-7407-4477-8bdd-50bdda545fe9","content":"<p>was</p>"},{"id":"d093713f-38b5-46c9-bd49-2817803a2aa4","content":"<p>have been</p>"},{"id":"6f1dd18a-ed2f-460a-80cc-0f59aa64e21c","content":"<p>were</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9d6b1c27-7407-4477-8bdd-50bdda545fe9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb "was" agrees in number with the singular subject "Farouk El-Baz."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb "are" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "Farouk El-Baz." Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb "have been" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "Farouk El-Baz." Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb "were" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "Farouk El-Baz."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-747-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$85261cf4-3aa7-4a0f-a6a1-661f590b9897$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"77bf77cd","external_id":"b426ef5f-b7cb-4486-bd1e-c1576e4ef344","vaultid":"a2555bb8-a0fa-458f-bfb0-b2099c413fb9","uId":"298d7f81-d77c-4992-bea4-9d8e0c2b82b1","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Farouk El-Baz, a geologist and space scientist, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> part of the team that selected the lunar landing sites for the Apollo program during the 1960s and 1970s.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a5a54cf8-21b4-4b93-bd9f-41a768b77772","content":"<p>are</p>"},{"id":"9d6b1c27-7407-4477-8bdd-50bdda545fe9","content":"<p>was</p>"},{"id":"d093713f-38b5-46c9-bd49-2817803a2aa4","content":"<p>have been</p>"},{"id":"6f1dd18a-ed2f-460a-80cc-0f59aa64e21c","content":"<p>were</p>"}],"keys":["9d6b1c27-7407-4477-8bdd-50bdda545fe9"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb \"was\" agrees in number with the singular subject \"Farouk El-Baz.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb \"are\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"Farouk El-Baz.\" Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb \"have been\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"Farouk El-Baz.\" Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb \"were\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject \"Farouk El-Baz.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-747-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"85261cf4-3aa7-4a0f-a6a1-661f590b9897","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959350,"updateDate":1691007959350}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959350, 1691007959350),
    ($SATQ$77e06a09$SATQ$, $SATQ$52f7b46f-3f44-4d76-8063-d928257b3164$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Between 322 and 184 BCE, the Maurya Empire established a complex economic system that, through trade and centralized <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> funded major infrastructure projects throughout the Indian subcontinent. This included the building of many roads, canals, and hospitals. &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1e340727-0a4b-430e-9aff-9f0efcc4c7ee","content":"<p>taxation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span></p>"},{"id":"ad57b6d5-b5d9-4eb4-b981-471c7d4eecd4","content":"<p>taxation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"a3cba6d9-8ab5-4f8a-9d23-5331fffd8057","content":"<p>taxation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">&mdash;</span></p>"},{"id":"429ada61-8ed8-40e1-9959-a6cc22fe6b37","content":"<p>taxation</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ad57b6d5-b5d9-4eb4-b981-471c7d4eecd4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. The comma after "taxation" pairs with the comma after "that" to separate the supplementary element "through trade and centralized taxation" from the rest of the sentence. This supplementary element functions to identify the funding source of the Mauryan economy, and the pair of commas indicates that this element could be removed without affecting the grammatical coherence of the sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice C is incorrect because a dash can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"77e06a09","external_id":"52f7b46f-3f44-4d76-8063-d928257b3164","vaultid":null,"uId":"3444769f-e07b-4982-bf3a-4b6e79b96436","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Between 322 and 184 BCE, the Maurya Empire established a complex economic system that, through trade and centralized <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> funded major infrastructure projects throughout the Indian subcontinent. This included the building of many roads, canals, and hospitals. &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1e340727-0a4b-430e-9aff-9f0efcc4c7ee","content":"<p>taxation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span></p>"},{"id":"ad57b6d5-b5d9-4eb4-b981-471c7d4eecd4","content":"<p>taxation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"},{"id":"a3cba6d9-8ab5-4f8a-9d23-5331fffd8057","content":"<p>taxation<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">&mdash;</span></p>"},{"id":"429ada61-8ed8-40e1-9959-a6cc22fe6b37","content":"<p>taxation</p>"}],"keys":["ad57b6d5-b5d9-4eb4-b981-471c7d4eecd4"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. The comma after \"taxation\" pairs with the comma after \"that\" to separate the supplementary element \"through trade and centralized taxation\" from the rest of the sentence. This supplementary element functions to identify the funding source of the Mauryan economy, and the pair of commas indicates that this element could be removed without affecting the grammatical coherence of the sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice C is incorrect because a dash can&rsquo;t be paired with a comma in this way to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect because it fails to use appropriate punctuation to separate the supplementary element from the rest of the sentence.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669207,"updateDate":1743430669207}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669207, 1743430669207),
    ($SATQ$78b88c04$SATQ$, $SATQ$244cf997-b270-4a5d-8ae4-1182edf85856$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$e69849dc-dc36-4b39-9255-34c4565d95ec$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Joshua Hinson, director of the language revitalization program of the Chickasaw Nation in Oklahoma, helped produce the world&rsquo;s first Indigenous-language instructional app, Chickasaw <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Chickasaw TV, in 2010; and a Rosetta Stone language course in Chickasaw, in 2015. </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"181f34cd-ae48-4789-8075-fd62bb3d9715","content":"<p>Basic; in 2009, an online television network;</p>"},{"id":"0aa3630d-2354-419c-a3c6-c540a3754a6b","content":"<p>Basic; in 2009, an online television network,</p>"},{"id":"54e15d6a-694c-4d8f-a60f-fc502cbc4bce","content":"<p>Basic, in 2009; an online television network,</p>"},{"id":"52384a89-a769-428b-a9ac-4feeda39d49b","content":"<p>Basic, in 2009, an online television network,&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["54e15d6a-694c-4d8f-a60f-fc502cbc4bce"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of items in a complex series. It&rsquo;s conventional to use a semicolon to separate items in a complex series with internal punctuation, and in this choice, the semicolon after &ldquo;2009&rdquo; is conventionally used to separate the first item (&ldquo;the world&rsquo;s&hellip;2009&rdquo;) and the second item (&ldquo;an online&hellip;2010&rdquo;) in the series of things that Hinson helped create. Further, the comma after &ldquo;Basic&rdquo; correctly pairs with the comma after &ldquo;app,&rdquo; and the comma after &ldquo;network&rdquo; correctly pairs with the comma after &ldquo;TV&rdquo; to set off the supplemental elements (&ldquo;Chickasaw Basic&rdquo; and &ldquo;Chickasaw TV&rdquo;) that provide the names of the app and the TV network, respectively. Altogether, the punctuation in this choice results in a sentence that clearly indicates that Hinson helped make a language app in 2009, an online TV network in 2010, and a language course in 2015. &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to punctuate the complex series in a way that makes clear that Hinson helped make a language app in 2009, an online TV network in 2010, and a language course in 2015. Choice B is incorrect because it fails to punctuate the complex series in a way that makes clear that Hinson helped make a language app in 2009, an online TV network in 2010, and a language course in 2015.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the comma after &ldquo;2009&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t match the semicolon used to separate the second and third items in the complex series. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-607-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$2c713773-6dd2-4e04-8f1b-3e4d7e81fee3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"78b88c04","external_id":"244cf997-b270-4a5d-8ae4-1182edf85856","vaultid":"e69849dc-dc36-4b39-9255-34c4565d95ec","uId":"faa6b18b-edb0-49df-b90f-a61a1ca4bde7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Joshua Hinson, director of the language revitalization program of the Chickasaw Nation in Oklahoma, helped produce the world&rsquo;s first Indigenous-language instructional app, Chickasaw <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Chickasaw TV, in 2010; and a Rosetta Stone language course in Chickasaw, in 2015. </p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"181f34cd-ae48-4789-8075-fd62bb3d9715","content":"<p>Basic; in 2009, an online television network;</p>"},{"id":"0aa3630d-2354-419c-a3c6-c540a3754a6b","content":"<p>Basic; in 2009, an online television network,</p>"},{"id":"54e15d6a-694c-4d8f-a60f-fc502cbc4bce","content":"<p>Basic, in 2009; an online television network,</p>"},{"id":"52384a89-a769-428b-a9ac-4feeda39d49b","content":"<p>Basic, in 2009, an online television network,&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["54e15d6a-694c-4d8f-a60f-fc502cbc4bce"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of items in a complex series. It&rsquo;s conventional to use a semicolon to separate items in a complex series with internal punctuation, and in this choice, the semicolon after &ldquo;2009&rdquo; is conventionally used to separate the first item (&ldquo;the world&rsquo;s&hellip;2009&rdquo;) and the second item (&ldquo;an online&hellip;2010&rdquo;) in the series of things that Hinson helped create. Further, the comma after &ldquo;Basic&rdquo; correctly pairs with the comma after &ldquo;app,&rdquo; and the comma after &ldquo;network&rdquo; correctly pairs with the comma after &ldquo;TV&rdquo; to set off the supplemental elements (&ldquo;Chickasaw Basic&rdquo; and &ldquo;Chickasaw TV&rdquo;) that provide the names of the app and the TV network, respectively. Altogether, the punctuation in this choice results in a sentence that clearly indicates that Hinson helped make a language app in 2009, an online TV network in 2010, and a language course in 2015. &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to punctuate the complex series in a way that makes clear that Hinson helped make a language app in 2009, an online TV network in 2010, and a language course in 2015. Choice B is incorrect because it fails to punctuate the complex series in a way that makes clear that Hinson helped make a language app in 2009, an online TV network in 2010, and a language course in 2015.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because the comma after &ldquo;2009&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t match the semicolon used to separate the second and third items in the complex series. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-607-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"2c713773-6dd2-4e04-8f1b-3e4d7e81fee3","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959339,"updateDate":1691007959339}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959339, 1691007959339),
    ($SATQ$78e978b5$SATQ$, $SATQ$01e4ee04-31c9-44d9-be3a-ab16ed00a0c1$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) tracks comparative price list data for its thirty-eight member countries. For instance, in July <span data-ssml-say-as="date">2021</span>, a hypothetical basket of goods priced at 100 US dollars (USD) in the United States would have cost 62 USD and 110 USD in fellow OECD <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> and Luxembourg, respectively.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1080f754-c15c-476f-b928-f804a3e46516","content":"<p>nations<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> Chile</p>"},{"id":"22b7a5f8-d2e4-41cd-9bbd-3df12f9f9b05","content":"<p>nations<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> Chile</p>"},{"id":"7c5e61ee-c721-472f-a568-991c6ca016b0","content":"<p>nations<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span> Chile</p>"},{"id":"866c2164-5e48-4587-a49b-59200c201120","content":"<p>nations Chile</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["866c2164-5e48-4587-a49b-59200c201120"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation between titles and proper nouns. No punctuation is needed to set off the coordinated noun phrase "Chile and Luxembourg" from the title that describes the two countries, "fellow OECD nations." Because the proper nouns "Chile and Luxembourg" are essential information identifying the "fellow OECD nations," no punctuation is necessary.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the country names ("Chile and Luxembourg") and the title ("fellow OECD nations") describing them. Further, setting the countries&rsquo; names off with commas suggests that they could be removed without affecting the coherence of the sentence, which isn&rsquo;t the case. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the country names ("Chile and Luxembourg") and the title ("fellow OECD nations") describing them. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the country names ("Chile and Luxembourg") and the title ("fellow OECD nations") describing them. Placing a colon after "nations" would confusingly suggest that these were the costs of the basket of goods for "fellow OECD nations" in general, not for Chile and Luxembourg specifically.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"78e978b5","external_id":"01e4ee04-31c9-44d9-be3a-ab16ed00a0c1","vaultid":null,"uId":"dda33c07-f311-4df4-ab47-7f524c4f235d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) tracks comparative price list data for its thirty-eight member countries. For instance, in July <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2021</span>, a hypothetical basket of goods priced at 100 US dollars (USD) in the United States would have cost 62 USD and 110 USD in fellow OECD <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> and Luxembourg, respectively.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1080f754-c15c-476f-b928-f804a3e46516","content":"<p>nations<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> Chile</p>"},{"id":"22b7a5f8-d2e4-41cd-9bbd-3df12f9f9b05","content":"<p>nations<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> Chile</p>"},{"id":"7c5e61ee-c721-472f-a568-991c6ca016b0","content":"<p>nations<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span> Chile</p>"},{"id":"866c2164-5e48-4587-a49b-59200c201120","content":"<p>nations Chile</p>"}],"keys":["866c2164-5e48-4587-a49b-59200c201120"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation between titles and proper nouns. No punctuation is needed to set off the coordinated noun phrase \"Chile and Luxembourg\" from the title that describes the two countries, \"fellow OECD nations.\" Because the proper nouns \"Chile and Luxembourg\" are essential information identifying the \"fellow OECD nations,\" no punctuation is necessary.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the country names (\"Chile and Luxembourg\") and the title (\"fellow OECD nations\") describing them. Further, setting the countries&rsquo; names off with commas suggests that they could be removed without affecting the coherence of the sentence, which isn&rsquo;t the case. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the country names (\"Chile and Luxembourg\") and the title (\"fellow OECD nations\") describing them. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the country names (\"Chile and Luxembourg\") and the title (\"fellow OECD nations\") describing them. Placing a colon after \"nations\" would confusingly suggest that these were the costs of the basket of goods for \"fellow OECD nations\" in general, not for Chile and Luxembourg specifically.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061175,"updateDate":1755117061175}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061175, 1755117061175),
    ($SATQ$790fc366$SATQ$, $SATQ$2e95b660-ad51-4543-8a1d-23e3151b4d55$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$6535533a-2c70-4f15-8b51-12ad01cd7ea9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Using satellite remote sensing, Dr. Catherine Nakalembe, director of NASA&rsquo;s Harvest Africa initiative, gathers important data on crop health. Nakalembe doesn&rsquo;t just compile the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> she also shares her findings with African farmers, enabling them to make data-driven decisions about managing critical food crops.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1005a7c7-dbae-41d9-8c01-63e1b0660fcc","content":"<p>information, though;</p>"},{"id":"e8666c1e-7048-49fa-ac01-aaffd3ff9280","content":"<p>information, though,</p>"},{"id":"09d1c710-eccd-4fca-8a81-850cb92fb3e6","content":"<p>information; though</p>"},{"id":"50564068-6705-43f7-8484-a2a2ec87a85b","content":"<p>information though,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1005a7c7-dbae-41d9-8c01-63e1b0660fcc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. This choice uses a semicolon to join two independent clauses ("Nakalembe doesn&rsquo;t just&hellip;though" and "she also shares..."). This choice also appropriately includes "though" in the first clause, where it logically belongs.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice results in a grammar error called a comma splice. It incorrectly joins two independent clauses with only a comma instead of a comma and a coordinating conjunction like "and" or "but." "Though" is a transition word, but it&rsquo;s not a coordinating conjunction. Choice C is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. A semicolon can only be used to link two independent clauses. However, if "though" is included in the second clause, it turns the second clause into a dependent clause, so a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used after "information." Choice D is incorrect. This choice results in a grammar error called a comma splice. It incorrectly joins two independent clauses with only a comma instead of a comma and a coordinating conjunction like "and" or "but."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-H008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$b128c541-2342-45cb-8496-19872f457ea8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"790fc366","external_id":"2e95b660-ad51-4543-8a1d-23e3151b4d55","vaultid":"6535533a-2c70-4f15-8b51-12ad01cd7ea9","uId":"66c6ca27-6b49-4b15-8be4-007d3c038db9","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Using satellite remote sensing, Dr. Catherine Nakalembe, director of NASA&rsquo;s Harvest Africa initiative, gathers important data on crop health. Nakalembe doesn&rsquo;t just compile the <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> she also shares her findings with African farmers, enabling them to make data-driven decisions about managing critical food crops.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1005a7c7-dbae-41d9-8c01-63e1b0660fcc","content":"<p>information, though;</p>"},{"id":"e8666c1e-7048-49fa-ac01-aaffd3ff9280","content":"<p>information, though,</p>"},{"id":"09d1c710-eccd-4fca-8a81-850cb92fb3e6","content":"<p>information; though</p>"},{"id":"50564068-6705-43f7-8484-a2a2ec87a85b","content":"<p>information though,</p>"}],"keys":["1005a7c7-dbae-41d9-8c01-63e1b0660fcc"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. This choice uses a semicolon to join two independent clauses (\"Nakalembe doesn&rsquo;t just&hellip;though\" and \"she also shares...\"). This choice also appropriately includes \"though\" in the first clause, where it logically belongs.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice results in a grammar error called a comma splice. It incorrectly joins two independent clauses with only a comma instead of a comma and a coordinating conjunction like \"and\" or \"but.\" \"Though\" is a transition word, but it&rsquo;s not a coordinating conjunction. Choice C is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. A semicolon can only be used to link two independent clauses. However, if \"though\" is included in the second clause, it turns the second clause into a dependent clause, so a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used after \"information.\" Choice D is incorrect. This choice results in a grammar error called a comma splice. It incorrectly joins two independent clauses with only a comma instead of a comma and a coordinating conjunction like \"and\" or \"but.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-H008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"b128c541-2342-45cb-8496-19872f457ea8","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959359,"updateDate":1691007959359}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959359, 1691007959359),
    ($SATQ$7a0d9031$SATQ$, $SATQ$eaca3bb7-2f15-424a-8bc5-e6a3c42557a8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b92f112a-b481-49b9-80ae-32fcb5ddbab9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In many of her landscape paintings from the 1970s and 1980s, Lebanese American artist Etel Adnan worked to capture the essence of California&rsquo;s fog-shrouded Mount Tamalpais region through abstraction, using splotches of color to represent the area&rsquo;s features. Interestingly, the triangle representing the mountain itself <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> among the few defined figures in her paintings.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1e000be6-f9f8-4a16-89f4-d53194ffa965","content":"<p>are</p>"},{"id":"e84c4cf7-b1cb-4cf8-a0e7-410a205efbd0","content":"<p>have been&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"0b3b657b-d357-4eed-860a-15a90415721f","content":"<p>were</p>"},{"id":"20207d47-e808-4f26-9ade-7aef27d121d9","content":"<p>is</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["20207d47-e808-4f26-9ade-7aef27d121d9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer.&nbsp;The convention being tested is subject&ndash;verb agreement. The singular verb &ldquo;is&rdquo; agrees in number with the singular subject &ldquo;the triangle.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;are&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;the triangle.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;have been&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;the triangle.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;were&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;the triangle.&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-622-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$43db6196-9001-4ec0-bd9c-ae8cc3dd9f43$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"7a0d9031","external_id":"eaca3bb7-2f15-424a-8bc5-e6a3c42557a8","vaultid":"b92f112a-b481-49b9-80ae-32fcb5ddbab9","uId":"b358ddb4-3fb5-4c9e-b8c4-7aea15824777","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In many of her landscape paintings from the 1970s and 1980s, Lebanese American artist Etel Adnan worked to capture the essence of California&rsquo;s fog-shrouded Mount Tamalpais region through abstraction, using splotches of color to represent the area&rsquo;s features. Interestingly, the triangle representing the mountain itself <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> among the few defined figures in her paintings.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1e000be6-f9f8-4a16-89f4-d53194ffa965","content":"<p>are</p>"},{"id":"e84c4cf7-b1cb-4cf8-a0e7-410a205efbd0","content":"<p>have been&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"0b3b657b-d357-4eed-860a-15a90415721f","content":"<p>were</p>"},{"id":"20207d47-e808-4f26-9ade-7aef27d121d9","content":"<p>is</p>"}],"keys":["20207d47-e808-4f26-9ade-7aef27d121d9"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer.&nbsp;The convention being tested is subject&ndash;verb agreement. The singular verb &ldquo;is&rdquo; agrees in number with the singular subject &ldquo;the triangle.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;are&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;the triangle.&rdquo; Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;have been&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;the triangle.&rdquo; Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb &ldquo;were&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject &ldquo;the triangle.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-622-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"43db6196-9001-4ec0-bd9c-ae8cc3dd9f43","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959339,"updateDate":1691007959339}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959339, 1691007959339)
